  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to use a combination of structured initialization and gradient-based optimization to find a better C5 bound. The previous methods used either random patterns or convex relaxations, but these may not efficiently navigate the complex non-convex landscape. The structured initialization provides a diverse starting point based on periodic patterns, while gradient-based optimization refines the solution with directional movement. This approach leverages the periodicity to balance overlaps across lags and uses local search to minimize the maximum overlap integral.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points} where 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h = n_points/2.\nObjective: Minimize the maximum over all lags k of \u222b h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx, scaled by dx = 2/n_points.\nConstraints:\n- For all i: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n- Overlap integral for lag k is approximated by sum_{i} h[i]*(1 - h[j]) * dx, where j = i +k (within bounds)\n\nThe objective is formulated as minimizing the maximum value of the discrete convolution between h and (1-h) scaled by dx. This involves computing the convolution for all lags, which corresponds to evaluating the overlap integral for every possible shift.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInputs: n_points (number of samples), initial_h_values (optional starting point)\nSteps:\na. Validate initial_h_values: If provided, resample to n_points using linear interpolation. Otherwise, generate a structured binary pattern:\n   - Create a binary array with n_points/2 1s placed periodically at intervals of m=ceil(n_points/2)\n   - Spread remaining 1s evenly across spacing gaps\n   - Normalize to ensure \u2211h = n_points/2\n\nb. Define objective function as the maximum of the convolution between h and (1-h), scaled by dx.\n\nc. Use a gradient-based optimizer (e.g., SLSQP) with:\n   - Bounds: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n   - Constraint: sum(h) = n_points/2\n   - Initial guess: structured binary pattern\n   - Iterations: 500\n   - Tolerance: ftol=1e-8\n\nd. During optimization:\n   - Clip values to [0,1] if they exceed bounds\n   - Rescale h to maintain the sum constraint\n   - Compute and track the maximum overlap integral\n\ne. Post-optimization:\n   - Validate that all h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n   - Recalculate C5 using the original evaluator to ensure consistency\n   - Store the best solution found\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault settings:\n- n_points = 256 for better resolution\n- Use periodic binary pattern with spacing ceil(n_points/2)\n- If initial_h_values provided, resample to match n_points using linear interpolation\n\nVariations:\n- If no parent provided, start with a random binary pattern with evenly spaced 1s\n- For faster computation, use smaller n_points (e.g., 128) with careful scaling\n- Run multiple optimizations with different parameter settings:\n   - Vary the number of iterations (250-750)\n   - Change the ftol (1e-8 to 1e-10)\n   - Adjust the initial pattern by shifting or scrambling the 1s\n- If solver fails, revert to structured initialization and re-solve\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime estimation:\n- Precompute overhead: negligible\n- Optimization execution: ~O(n_points^2) in time complexity\n- 256 points: ~65536 operations, within 600-second budget\n\nSafety measures:\n- Track best solution found during iterations\n- On solver failure, fallback to structured initialization and re-solve\n- If no improvement for 30 seconds, terminate and return best found\n- If constraints are violated, apply a repair step by projecting to the feasible set\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nPost-solution checks:\n- Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] with absolute tolerance < 1e-6\n- Verify sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 with absolute error < 1e-6\n- Recalculate C5 with original evaluator to match exact metric\n- Check that maximum lag value exceeds all other lags\n- Ensure returned h is a NumPy array of correct shape and type\n\nSanity checks:\n- Verify no division by zero or invalid indices\n- Ensure the maximum overlap integral is correctly computed\n- Validate that the initial and final solutions are compatible\n- If a parent is provided, ensure the final solution is a refined version of it",
